Yes. The healthiest version is acceptance and aspiration at the same time.

The trick is not making her feel, “I’ll be worthy when I fix this.” It’s helping create the opposite frame: “My body is already mine, already valuable, and because I value it, I want to take good care of it and see what it can become.”

That changes improvement from punishment into stewardship. Training, nutrition, style, sleep, confidence, sexuality, all of it becomes an expression of ownership rather than a war against herself.

And if you’re trying to support that in someone else, the strongest signals are usually: admire what is already there, reinforce capability and effort, avoid comparison, and never make affection conditional on improvement.

A concise formulation would be:

Love the body as home; improve it as craft.